  "prediction": "**John Lewis (TAO-205) Class Oiler Shipbuilding Program: Background and Issues for Congress**\n\nThe John Lewis (TAO-205) class oiler shipbuilding program is a Navy initiative to build a new class of 20 fleet oilers to support the Navy's operations. The program aims to replace the existing 15 Henry J. Kaiser (TAO-187) class ships, which have an expected service life of 35 years. The Navy's proposed FY2020 budget requests the procurement of the fifth and sixth ships in the program.\n\n**Key Issues for Congress:**\n\n1. **Procurement Funding:** Congress must decide whether to approve, reject, or modify the Navy's FY2020 procurement funding request for the TAO-205 program, which totals $1,057.9 million.\n2. **Number of Oilers Required:** The Navy's operational concept, Distributed Maritime Operations (DMO), may require more oilers to support its operations. The Navy has not assessed the effects of DMO on its logistics requirements.\n3. **Ship Self-Defense Equipment:** Congress must consider whether to encourage or direct the Navy to build TAO-205s with more ship self-defense equipment than currently planned, which could increase procurement costs.\n\n**Program Status:**\n\n* The first TAO-205 ship is scheduled for delivery in November 2020.\n* The Navy has matured all critical technologies and stabilized the ship's design.\n* The program office has largely kept to its construction schedule, but a flooding incident at a NASSCO graving dock in July 2018 has affected the delivery of future ships.\n\n**Recommendations:**\n\n* Congress should carefully review the Navy's procurement funding request and consider the potential impact of DMO on logistics requirements.\n* The Navy should assess the effects of DMO on its logistics requirements and provide a plan to address any shortfalls.\n* Congress should consider directing the Navy to build TAO-205s with more ship self-defense equipment to ensure the ships can operate safely in a contested environment.",
